WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.575
Drone operations are the planned and controlled use of an unmanned aircraft system to accomplish a defined purpose.

00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:19.788
The system includes more than the aircraft: it also includes the controller, software, communications, batteries, sensors, payloads, records, and people responsible for the flight.

00:00:19.788 --> 00:00:27.923
Operator roles may include a pilot in command, visual observer, payload operator, mission lead, or data reviewer.

00:00:27.923 --> 00:00:33.420
Even a small recreational flight concentrates several responsibilities in one person.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.796
Each role needs a shared understanding of the plan, limits, and emergency actions.

00:00:39.796 --> 00:00:47.052
Drones support recreation, inspection, mapping, photography, research, public safety, security, and many other legitimate uses.

00:00:47.052 --> 00:00:57.386
The same aircraft may be suitable for one mission and inappropriate for another because weather, airspace, people, payload, or data sensitivity differ.

00:00:57.386 --> 00:01:00.244
Successful operation begins before takeoff.

00:01:00.244 --> 00:01:13.216
The operator defines the purpose, verifies the aircraft and crew are ready, reviews the location and current official requirements, assesses hazards, and selects conservative limits for the mission.

00:01:13.216 --> 00:01:16.954
Safety, legality, and accountability are core principles.

00:01:16.954 --> 00:01:23.330
Technical capability does not create permission to fly anywhere or collect any data.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:31.905
Operators must respect aviation rules, property, privacy, people, other aircraft, and the limits of their equipment and training.

00:01:31.905 --> 00:01:37.402
The goal is a useful flight completed without creating unacceptable risk.

00:01:37.402 --> 00:01:48.175
Good operators are willing to delay, change, or cancel a mission when conditions, authorization, equipment, or crew readiness do not support safe operation.
